WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.580
To be famous in the world of esports, it’s safe to say you need to be able to appreciate

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:14.620
a good meme.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:24.140
True super stardom in this world, doesn’t take place in big press conferences, red carpets

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.980
or glamorous photo shoots.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.720
In esports you’ve got to master the art of Reddit.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:35.500
The combination of being absolutely incredible at your game of choice while simultaneously

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.440
not taking anything seriously.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:53.940
(Stream)

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:59.400
Maybe nobody in esports history embodies this more than the King of Reddit himself:

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.760
ShroudyRowdy

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:06.760
From clutching on the biggest stage with Cloud 9 to the top of the Twitch charts.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.180
This is his story.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.200
Shroud joined Cloud9’s Counter Strike: Global Offensive roster on

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:24.360
September 1, 2014, after the organization acquired compLexity’s roster, which featured

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.369
Shroud as a stand-in.

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:32.090
Shroud was a streamer who had been competing in ESEA qualifiers with Exertus eSports and

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.140
Manajuma during 2013 and early 2014.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:43.060
It was during this era that he impressed the eventual Cloud9 roster and landed himself a spot on the squad.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:48.660
"The main way I kinda got scouted was I streamed a lot. Streaming is actually a good way for people to

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.180
scout I guess. Because they can watch your stream."

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:56.980
"I didn't even know I was going to join the team, I just finished the regular season with Manajumas.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:04.260
And then a week after Dboorn messages me and says, "Hey do you want to join Complexity?"

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.200
Which is Cloud9 now and I was just like, of course yeah."

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:14.040
From his beginnings in Mississauga, Canada, Shroud was bred to play games.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:20.640
He lived with his father, who built computers obsessively and introduced Shroud to games as a child.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.040
"I love games, I love computers. I built every single computer we owned.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:34.040
And since he was really tiny I was putting him on my lap and let him do whatever.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:39.880
And then I introduced him to Counter-Strike because I like that game, and he picked up so quickly that

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:43.560
after a couple of months there was pointless for me to play with him.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:49.520
Shroud started streaming while pro Counter-Strike was still a pipe dream for him in 2011.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:56.560
He’d actually lost interest in CS during Source, but his FPS fire was rejuvenated when

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.420
a friend gifted him CSGO on Steam a year or so later.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:06.620
Shroud was a huge fan of Summit1G on Twitch and wanted to give streaming a try.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:12.380
"So I first started streaming, oh boy it was probably like 2011ish.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:18.440
I didn't have any viewers, I streamed probably for a year with 1 viewer and that was myself.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:23.980
And the reason I started streaming, was because of Summit. And I was watching him play and he had

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:30.660
a couple hundred viewers and it was just how much he streamed inspired me, he quit his job and whatnot

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.680
It was just- I wanted to do that."

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:39.800
It was a long time before he saw any success on the platform, but his pro career in CS:GO allowed

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.800
his stream to grow.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:49.000
Shroud made his LAN debut with Cloud9 in 2015 at ESL ESEA Pro League North America Season 1.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:56.160
Shroud and C9 won the NA bracket and then finished second to Fnatic in the international finals.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:37.800
(Casting)

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:43.100
In an era where North America was considered the minor leagues of pro CS,

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.860
C9 making it to the Pro League finals was huge.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.400
Cloud 9’s summer run in 2015 was big for NA CS

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:57.660
and Shroud was quickly building a reputation for being one of the most talented players in North America.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:02.400
"Cloud9 looked at Shroud and they saw potential. They liked his decision making, they liked his positioning.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.880
And they said "Okay if we can develop this guy if we  give him more experience.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.880
We like how he's playing the game, but if we give him more time to develop with good teammates, those

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.840
frags will come." The frags aren't the issue for them. They saw how stellar his aim was, how pinpoint and

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:15.920
and precise it was."

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.300
"I love Shroud to death and I think he's one of the best players I've ever played with man.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:24.700
Probably the best, best pure aimer I've ever seen in my life."

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:29.680
"Shroud is literally like the best aimer in North America, like hands down I feel like when he's rifling."

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.120
"Like you can watch this guy on stream pull off the most ridiculous 4ks and 5ks."

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:37.600
After their ESL Pro League finish in 2015,

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:43.640
Cloud 9 and Shroud’s next big finish was at DreamHack Bucharest in 2016.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:49.840
Once again they were the runner-up, falling to another one of the world’s strongest teams in Virtus.Pro.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:06:13.160
(Casting)

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:19.940
During the latter half of 2016 Shroud and Cloud 9 finally won a big tournament.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:40.220
(Casting)

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:45.820
C9 defeated SK Gaming in the Finals of ESL Pro League Season 4,

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:49.440
to bring a significant title to American Counter Strike.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.660
That fateful summer of Cloud9 was capped-off with a bang.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:59.040
And this team was single-handedly making a case for North America’s potential

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:01.680
to compete on the international stage.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:06.120
At this point, Shroud had been relegated to more of a supporting role

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:09.660
than his early days of wowing on stage with his aim.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.540
"I'm trying to help those guys f*cking be the all-stars.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:15.540
I'm no longer the all-star.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:18.720
For those of you that don't already know.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:22.320
My role isn't to f***ing make plays and all that bullsh*t.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.740
It's to help them make plays.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.040
I'll die for them I'll flash for them, I'll f***ing smoke for them, I'll do anything for those guys."

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.180
"I don't care how much sh*t anyone's gonna give me."

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.300
With the injection of youth into C9’s roster,

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.760
Shroud was doing a lot of lurking in-game and mentoring the new players.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:47.120
The addition of Stewie2k and Autimatic to the classic C9 core of Shroud,

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:52.420
N0thing and Skadoodle proved that an American team could play at the highest level.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:56.540
But the real test, was competing at a big time CSGO event

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:59.140
where all the best teams showed up.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:38.100
(Casting)

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:42.600
Cloud9 just barely squeaked into the elimination rounds at 3-2

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:48.300
after opening with back-to-back losses to Ninjas in Pyjamas and Natus Vincere.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:52.200
In the bracket stage there were three North American hopefuls:

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:55.140
C9, Liquid and OpTic.

00:08:55.900 --> 00:09:01.420
But after Cloud 9 got their revenge on NiP in the quarters, they were NA’s last hope.

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:05.960
Cloud 9’s cinderella run continued when they faced off against Na’Vi.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:11.000
They showed up in a big way and trounced Na’Vi in a 2-0 victory.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:38.920
(Casting)

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:45.200
A North American team was about to play David against a Goliath in SK Gaming,

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:49.820
and there was a $100,000 grand prize hanging in the balance.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:10:37.820
(Casting)

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:40.680
Cloud 9 might not have won it all.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:46.340
But they proved once again that they had what it takes to compete at the game’s highest level

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:49.960
Shroud was instrumental in C9’s run in Cologne,

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:53.440
particularly in the bracket stage when it counted most.

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:56.580
Of course after Shroud left C9,

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:01.120
they went on to become the first North American team to ever win a Major title.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:05.580
But, his impact on that run in Boston is notable nonetheless.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:10.440
Shroud was a key cog in what would come to be America’s CS:GO darlings

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:14.400
and without his consistency, game IQ and commitment

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:16.500
they wouldn’t be the team we know today.

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:21.500
Even Stewie admitted that Shroud was one of the things that made him want to be a pro.

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:27.860
"So when I watched streamers, like I used to watch Mike play a lot. And I would see him do certain

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:33.220
aim- like a certain way to aim, then I would try to mimic it. I would try to steal people's play style

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:34.320
and add it to my own."

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:38.720
Throughout his career as a pro player Shroud balanced his career

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:41.180
with the growth and development of his stream.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:50.400
(Stream)

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:54.560
What could be better, right? Streamer by night, pro rifler by day.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:12:00.780
But at times the Counter Strike community were critical of Shroud for his career juggling act.

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:05.660
"Unluckily for Shroud he had unfortunately not the strongest performance this weekend in the Summit.

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:11.300
And he got roasted it looks like a little bit by the community because he streams other games."

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:12.500
"Motherf*cker."

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:15.020
"He doesn't just play CS every hour of the day."

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:18.640
When his performance waned or Cloud 9 were struggling,

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:24.380
fans wanted to see Shroudy practicing CS — not playing other games on stream.

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:30.600
"Dude mods, like literally. My mods feel free to ban to f*cking ban anyone just crying about

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:35.480
what other people say. Like I don't care to see that,

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:37.560
I don't care to see any of that sh*t."

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:39.060
"What?"

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:40.900
"Do you still like playing Counter-Strike?"

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:42.360
"No. Dead game."

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:48.920
"When people criticize pro players for not purely just practicing whatever their field or sport or game is

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:49.580
all the time."

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:55.040
"So at the moment, the instance that's really brought this up to me, is the case where people are really

00:12:55.040 --> 00:13:00.900
critiquing Shroud over the fact that he'll stream PUB Battlegrounds or whatever the f*ck."

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:05.320
"The idea that you know he's doing that instead of practicing and that's why he's not some top player

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:06.040
or whatever."

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:10.560
"You haven't seen the CS reddit Shroud, it used to be all praise for you. Now they hate you."

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:14.700
With that said, even at the height of his career,

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:19.940
Shroud was more popular as a streamer and personality, than as a pro player.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:26.960
And after C9’s run in Cologne in 2017, Shroud started to focus more than ever on streaming.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:33.780
And this aligned nicely, with the explosion of one of the biggest games of the last few years.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:38.040
PlayerUnknown’s Battlegrounds was released in March 2017,

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:43.540
and by the time the summer rolled around, was taking the gaming world by storm.

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:47.100
A few months prior to his choice to step down from C9,

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:54.100
Shroud hopped into PUBG. And, well — he was unbelievably good at it.

00:13:54.100 --> 00:14:27.060
(Stream)

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:33.300
And then, in August 2017 Shroud stepped back from the team’s starting roster

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:36.760
and decided to focus entirely on his stream.

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:43.640
"I messaged Jack and I was like "Hey, (Jack's the owner of C9 by the way) I kinda want to step down.

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:49.300
I would love to be your guys' backup, I want to see where full-time stream takes me."

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:55.380
"It felt like it was about time for me to go, like this whole PUBG thing came out,

00:14:55.440 --> 00:15:00.400
I've enjoyed PUBG so much and I'm like slowly losing my passion to compete.

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:03.560
I just want to relax, sit here, and game."

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:08.300
During that same August, PUBG had its biggest streaming month.

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:14.120
The game was streamed to an average of around 100,000 viewers at a time.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:19.200
As Shroud lept into streaming and left the safety of his pro career behind,

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:25.440
he and PUBG were attached at the hip, and both were only getting bigger.

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:33.480
In August 2017, the first month he streamed full time. He averaged at just under 20,000 viewers at a time.

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:41.340
Since then, he's never averaged less and peaked in April of 2018, with an average of 40,000 viewers.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:45.180
"I'm literally like shaking."

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:57.460
"Like this is crazy, I'm about to be at 7500 subs. You guys... Oh my god the subs."

00:15:57.460 --> 00:16:00.840
"Oh my god I look over to my right and it's just a wall."

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:03.000
"Oh my god."

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:08.760
Professional esports and streaming have operated adjacent to one another for years now.

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:14.420
not surprising for your favorite pro player to also dip their foot into Twitch

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:16.040
to help build their brand.

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:22.960
But as of late, we’ve seen streamers whose Twitch personality surpasses any fame or fandom

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:24.980
they’d achieve as a pro player.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:32.920
Figures like Ninja and Dr.Disrespect have never been in the forefront of any major professional esport

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:36.560
but have become some of gaming’s biggest stars.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:40.000
And Shroud wanted to give himself a chance at that.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:43.540
At being more than an above average pro Counter Strike player.

00:16:43.580 --> 00:17:12.560
(Stream)

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:17.340
Even while he was a pro player, Shroud was always known as the king of Reddit.

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:20.640
But, since his commitment to full-time streaming,

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:23.740
he’s earned that nickname tenfold.

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:28.540
"Watch this..."

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:39.400
(Stream)

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:43.640
Other top broadcasters fill up your computer screen with music,

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:46.400
gimmicks and boisterous entertainment.

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:49.620
But Shroud, is just a straight-up gamer.

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:53.260
And he’s damn good at every game he plays.

00:17:53.440 --> 00:18:12.680
(Stream)

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:18.400
"I always pick Shroud's mind cause I consider Shroud, the best PC player on Twitch."

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:27.480
"But when you wanna talk about PC and PC precision, I think Shroud is just miles in front of anybody."

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:33.820
In a lot of ways, Shroud has become a sanctum, for the old guard of esports fans to kick back

00:18:33.820 --> 00:18:35.080
and hang out.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:41.220
He hasn't adapted to the overly boisterous style of other massive streamers like Ninja.

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:47.260
Shroud is just a great gamer who streams every day, and he owns that brand.

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:51.600
And it’s that rock solid brand that he’s built his community around.

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:55.880
Shroud has become the place to go for esports fans, redditers

00:18:55.900 --> 00:19:00.220
and anyone looking to watch high level gameplay and grab a laugh.

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:25.600
(Stream)

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:31.060
What cements his style even more is his consistent support of small streamers.

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:35.260
He's found a way to build content, out of helping people.

00:19:35.360 --> 00:20:27.080
(Stream)

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:30.680
No matter the game, Shroud’s viewership stays strong

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:34.660
something that’s unusual for all but the biggest streamers.

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:42.940
Shroud has recently streamed CSGO, PUBG, Fortnite, Realm Royale and World of Warcraft

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:46.560
Somehow, he’s just great at every game he tries.

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:49.400
Overshadowed by his growth a streamer

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:54.080
is the fact that Shroud has become one of the best Player Unknown’s Battleground players on the planet.

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:59.900
"But I was watching Shroud last night, play Battlegrounds. In first person."

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:04.620
"That guy's a maniac."

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:08.740
"He might be the best player in the game."

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:14.400
Recently, in a team tournament, Shroud and another popular streamer Chadd

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:19.020
took down 9 out of 10 winner winner chicken dinners in the event.

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:35.840
(Stream)

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:38.920
In a lot of ways, the popularity of Fortnite

00:21:39.080 --> 00:21:42.920
has epitomized why Shroud’s viewers are so loyal to him.

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:45.620
He knows he could give in to the hot new thing,

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:50.320
but in typical Shroud fashion he’s stayed loyal to PUBG.

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:56.380
He’s not trying to be like Ninja or ride the Fortnite wave into mainstream success.

00:21:56.900 --> 00:22:01.040
Shroud has truly lived the esports rollercoaster.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:06.900
He’s evolved from North American Counter-Strike golden boy to Reddit’s darling

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:08.620
right before our eyes.

00:22:08.980 --> 00:22:13.720
There’s no question that there’s a long way to go in esports’ fight to legitimize

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:20.920
itself in the mainstream and offer long term career options past the upper-echelon of each game.

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:25.440
But, if there’s a benchmark to follow when it comes to remaining relevant throughout

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:29.540
your career in a world where there’s new games popping up left and right

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:31.580
Shroud is it.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:33.160
He’s adapted.

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:34.920
He’s stayed loyal.

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:42.600
And he’s still here, growing and leaving his mark on every aspect of this community we love.

00:22:45.400 --> 00:22:50.440
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

